WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.910
Build, test and protect a dashboard an owner can trust.

2
00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:08.530
This is the video for module two of Advanced Excel and Dashboards.

3
00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:20.510
Most dashboards are fed by exports from a POS system, a bank or accounting software. Cleaning them by hand every month is slow, and it is where errors creep in.

4
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.770
You will build one screen that answers the owner's repeated questions.

5
00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:34.150
A wrong number that looks right is worse than an obvious error, because people act on it. So you will prove every number.

6
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.770
And you will protect and document the file so someone else can run it without you.

7
00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:50.510
Ada is admin officer for a pharmacy with three branches in Abuja. She saves every branch export in one folder called POS Exports.

8
00:00:51.760 --> 00:01:00.790
Power Query stacks the files into one query. Every step she takes is recorded under Applied Steps, and her original files are never changed.

9
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:06.390
She removes the 4 title lines and promotes the first row to headers.

10
00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:16.360
She sets Amount as a number, trims spaces from names, and replaces PCM 500 with Paracetamol 500mg.

11
00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:36.200
She loads it to a Table and checks the row count. January's three reports give 2,140 plus 1,875 plus 1,310, which is 5,325 rows. The query shows 5,325.

12
00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:51.270
Watch the date trap. Some computers read 03/04 as 4 March. Set the date type Using Locale, English Nigeria, and check a date like 13/05 in the result.

13
00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:55.220
Next month, she saves three new files and clicks Refresh All.

14
00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:05.820
Put three to five KPI cards across the top left, where the eye lands first. Trends go beneath, then breakdowns, then detail lists.

15
00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:20.660
Sales this month are 18,600,000 naira against 17,200,000 last month. Percentage change divides by the previous period, so the change is 8.1 percent.

16
00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:33.240
Gross profit is 4,650,000, so the margin is 25 percent. Always calculate margin from totals, never by averaging item margins.

17
00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:43.850
6,200 transactions give an average basket of 3,000 naira. A fourth card counts 14 items below reorder level.

18
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:53.230
Use a line chart for sales over months, and a bar chart sorted largest to smallest for branches. Avoid 3D effects.

19
00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:03.330
Insert a Branch slicer and a Date timeline. Use Report Connections to tick every pivot, so one click on Kubwa filters the whole screen.

20
00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:11.710
FILTER can list every unpaid account over 30 days. Leave the cells below it empty, or you get a spill error.

21
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:20.720
Some readers cannot tell red from green, so add a plus or minus sign, and give each chart a title that states the message.

22
00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:28.030
Create a Checks area. Each check compares two numbers that must agree, and the difference should be 0.

23
00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:39.270
Ada's source Table adds up to 18,912,500 naira, but the dashboard shows 18,600,000.

24
00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:57.180
The gap is 312,500. The pivot pointed to a fixed range, so 5 late invoices of 62,500 each were left out. She points the pivot at tblSales, refreshes, and the check returns 0.

25
00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:09.540
Add data validation to input cells, unlock only those cells, then protect the sheet. Test that the slicers still work. But sheet protection only prevents accidents.

26
00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:19.110
For salaries, customer details or bank information, use Encrypt with Password, with a strong password sent through a different channel.

27
00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:28.460
Exports often hold customer names and phone numbers. Remove columns you do not need, and never forward raw exports on personal WhatsApp.

28
00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:39.230
Before you send the file, tick every item. Test with awkward data on purpose: a blank quantity, a date typed as text, a duplicate invoice.

29
00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:45.470
Do not hide errors with IFERROR and a zero, because a broken lookup then looks like a real zero.

30
00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:50.510
Finally, write a How It Works sheet and ask someone else to follow it without your help.

31
00:04:51.760 --> 00:05:04.410
Automate an import from a folder of at least three files. Build your one-screen dashboard with cards, charts and a connected slicer. Then add check cells, protect it and write the How It Works sheet.

32
00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.920
Now continue with the lessons below.
